Here's how to solve the problem:

First, note that the summand is the reciprocal of a geometric series with first term $a=1$ and common ratio $r=\omega^3.$ Since the terms are not mutual reciprocals, we don't have a geometric series in the strict sense, but we can rearrange the terms to make one. Define the summand as $s_k$ and let $t_k = s_k - s_{k+1}.$ Then we have
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^{1728} s_k & = t_1 + (t_1 + t_2) + (t_1 + t_2 + t_3) + \dots + (t_1 + t_2 + t_3 + \dots + t_{1728}) \\
& = t_1 (1 + \underbrace{1 + \dots + 1}_{1728} )
\end{align*}
where the inner sum equals 1728, the number of terms in the original sum. Therefore, we want to find $t_1.$

To do this, first note that, for any integer $n,$ we have $\omega^n + \omega^{-n} = 2\cos (2\pi n/1729).$ So our summand can be written as
\begin{align*}
s_k & = \frac{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omega^{-k} + \omega^{2k} + \omega^{-2k}} \\
& = \frac{1}{2\left(1 + \cos(2\pi k/1729)\right)} \text{ (setting $x = \sin(\pi k/864)$ and using the double-angle formula)}\\
& = \frac{1}{2(1 + \cos \pi x /4)}\text{ (since $k/1729 = 8n/864$ for some integer $n$)}.
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*}
t_k & = s_k - s_{k+1} \\
& = \frac{1}{2(1 + \cos (\pi x / 4))} - \frac{1}{2(1 + \cos (\pi x/4 + \pi / 1729))}\\
& = \frac{\cos(\pi x/4) - \cos (\pi x / 4 + \pi / 1729)}{\left(1 + \cos (\pi x/4)(1 + \cos(\pi x / 4 + \pi / 1729))\right)}
\end{align*}
Letting $x = 0$ will give $t_1.$ After some calculations, we find that
\begin{align*}
t_1 = \frac{1719}{250871584} & = \frac{446}{6264579}\text{ (reducing fraction)}\\
& = \frac{2}{6264579}
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^{1728}\frac{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omega^{-k} + \omega^{2k} + \omega^{-2k}} & = t_1(1 + 1 + \cdots + 1) \\
& = \frac{2 (1 + 1 + \cdots + 1)}{6264579}
\end{align*}
Since there are 1728 terms on the left, the sum of 1728 1's is 1728. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^{1728}\frac{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omega^{-k} + \omega^{2k} + \omega^{-2k}} & = \frac{2 \cdot 1728}{6264579} \\
& = \frac{3456}{6264579} \\
