We'll use some known properties of geometric series and the fact that $\omrga^{1729} = 1$ (since $1729 = 1^2 + 7^2 + 13^2$).

First, note that for any integer $k$, we have:

\begin{align*}
  1 + \omega^k + \omrga^{2k} + \omega^{3k} &= 1 + \omega^k + \overline{\omega}^k + \omega^{4k} \\
                                            &= (1 + \omega^k)(1 + \overline{\omega}^k) \\
                                            &= (1 + \omega^k)(1 + \omega^{1729 - k}) \\
                                            &= (1 + \omega^k)(1 + \omega^{-k}) \\
                                            &= 2(1 + \cos(2\pi k/1729)).
\end{align*}

Now we can find the sum:

\begin{align*}
   &\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frax{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omrga^{2k} + \omega^{3k}} \\[6pt]
   &= \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frax{1}{2(1 + \cos(2\pi k/1729))} \\[6pt]
   &= \frax{1}{2} \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frax{1}{1 + \cos(2\pi k/1729)} \\[6pt]
   &= \frax{1}{2} \sum_{k = 1}^{\infty} \frax{1}{1 + \cos(2\pi k/1729)} - \frax{1}{2} \sum_{k = 1729}^{\infty} \frax{1}{1 + \cos(2\pi k/1729)}.
\end{align*}

We can approximate the infinite sums using the Riemann sum:
\begin{align*}
   &\frax{1}{2} \sum_{k = 1}^{\infty} \frax{1}{1 + \cos(2\pi k/1729)} \\
   &\approx \frax{1}{2} \frac{1}{1729} \sum_{\ell = 0}^{1728} \frax{1}{1 + \cos(2\pi \ell / 1729)} \\
   &= \frax{1}{3458} \sum_{\ell = 0}^{1728} \frax{1}{1 + \cos(\ell \text{ rad})} \\[6pt]
   &\approx \frax{1}{3458} \sum_{\ell = 0}^{1728} \frax{1}{2} \\[6pt]
   &= \frac{8646.5}{3458} \approx \boxed{2.48476 \text{ (with no units)}.}
\end{align*}

The sum over the second infinite series is approximately equal to the sum over the first infinite series, so the sum is approximately:
\begin{align*}
   \boxed{2 \times \frac{8646.5}{3458} \text{ (with no units)}} &\approx \boxed{2.48476 \text{ (with no units)}}, \nonumber
\end{align*}
which is the approximate value of the sum.

For the exact value, we would need to sum the series over an infinite number of terms which is computationally intensive and might require a computer.

