\| A - B \| &= 8
\end{align*}

2) Since a diameter of a circle divides the circle into two equal semicircles, each semicircle has radius $r = \frac{diameter}{2} = \frac{8}{2} = 4$.

3) Next, we calculate the area of a semicircle using the formula:  \begin{align*}
Area \, of \, semicircle &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \pi \cdot r^2 \\
Area \, of \, semicircle &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \pi \cdot (4)^2 \\
Area \, of \, semicircle &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \pi \cdot 16 \\
Area \, of \, semicircle &= 8 \cdot \pi
\end{align*}
Since there are two semicircles in the entire circle, we double the area of one semicircle to find the area of the full circle:
4) \begin{align*}
Area \, of \, entire \, circle &= 2 \cdot Area \, of \, semicircle \\
Area \, of \, entire \, circle &= 2 \cdot (8 \cdot \pi) \\
Area \, of \, entire \, circle &= 16 \cdot \pi
\end{align*}

5) Since the area of the circle is equal to the area of the square inscribed within the circle, calculate the area of the inscribed square by squaring the diameter of the circle:
\begin{align*}
Area \, of \, square &= (diameter)^2 \\
Area \, of \, square &= (8)^2 \\
Area \, of \, square &= 64
\end{align*}

6) The area of the circle is equal to the area of the square, so:
\begin{align*}
Area \, of \, square &= Area \, of \, entire \, circle \\
64 &= 16 \cdot \pi \\
\frac{64}{16} &= \pi
\end{align*}
From this equation, we find that $\pi \approx 64 / 16 = 4$. Now that we know $\pi$ is 4, we can re-Calculate the area of the circle using this updated value of $\pi$:
\begin{align*}
Area \, of \, entire \, circle &= 16 \cdot \pi \\
Area \, of \, entire \, circle &= 16 \cdot 4 \\
Area \, of \, entire \, circle &= 64
\end{align*}

7) However, we made a mistake in our initial calculation of $\pi$. The correct value of $\pi$ is approximately 3.14159. Let's correct our calculation using this new value of $\pi$:
\begin{align*}
