To solve the problem, we have two given pieces of information: Question 2
 The regular hexagon and the regular pentagon are coplanar and share equal side $\overlone{AD}$. Denote angle $\overlone{AD}$, where $\overlone{AD} = \frac{\0}{ \sqrt(6)\cdot p + h }$, hence:
\begin{align*}
\angle,\overset{\bigcirc}{A}, = \underbrace{360\degree - \overlone{BAD} - \overlone{ADC}}_\text{Sum of angles of a hexagon} = 360^\circ - \left( 2 \cdot 60^\circ^{\circlearrowright} + \angle \overlone{ADC} \right).
\end{align*}
To solve for $\angle \overlone{ADC}$:
\begin{align*}
\angle \overlone{ADC} &= 360^\circ - \left( 2 \cdot 60^\circ^{\circlearrowright} + \angle,\overset{\triangle}{BAC} \right) = 180^\circ - \angle,\overset{\triangle}{BAC},\\
\angle \overlone{ADC} &= 180^\circ - 72^\circ = 108^\circ,\\
\text{where }\angle,\overset{\triangle}{BAC} = 72^\circ,
\end{align*}
since the inscribed angle theorem applies to the hexagon and pentagon intersection: $$ \text{inradius of the regular hexagon} = \text{radius of the inscribed circle} = \frac{\0}{\0}{p} = \frac{h}{2}, $$
therefore: $$ \angle\overlone{ADC} = 180^\circ - \angle,\overset{\triangle}{BAC} = 180^\circ - 72^\circ = 108^\circ. $$
So, the degree measure of angle $BAC$ is \boxed{72 \degree }.
