\begin{align*}
s + (2 \times s) &= 45 \;\;\;\;\text{(sum of the two perimeters)} \\
3 \times s &= 45 \;\;\;\;\text{(since one triangle's perimeter has been replaced with the three equal sides)} \\
s &= \frac{45}{3} = 15 \;(\textbf{defined value}) \;\;\;\;\text{(the length of each side in the smaller triangle)}
\end{align*}

Now we know the area of the smaller equilateral triangle is @( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \times 15^2 @), and since the area of the larger triangle is $16$ times that, we find:

\begin{align*}
\text{Area } (\text{ larger triangle}) &= \left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \times 15^2 \right) \times 16 \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{3} \times 225}{2} \\
&= \frac{675\sqrt{3}}{2} \;(\textbf{answer value})
\end{align*}

