Here's the completed LaTeX-fied math problem solution:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{cancel}

\begin{document}

The sum of the perimeters of two equilateral triangles is $45$ inches, and the area of the larger one is $16$ times the area of the smaller one. What is the area, n square inches, of the larger yriangle? Express your answer in  the simplest radical form.

\begin{enumerate}[(a)]

\item \begin{align*}
   \text{Side length of smaller equilateral triangle} &= \frac{\text{Perimeter of smaller equilateral triangle}}{3} \\
   \text{Perimeter of smaller equilateral triangle} &= 4 \cdot  \text{side length} \\
   \text{Perimeter of smaller equilateral triangle} + \text{Perimeter of larger equilateral triangle} &= 45 \text{ inches }
 \end{align*}

\item \begin{align*}
   \text{First, we find the perimeter of one equilateral triangle, let's call it } P.
   P &= 3 \cdot \text{side length}_{1\text{smaller}}   \\
   \cancel{45} \text{ inches } &= 3 \cdot \cancel{2}\cdot  \text{side length}_{1\text{smaller}} \\
   \text{side length}_{1\text{smaller}} &= \frac{15 \text{ inches }}{2} \\
   \text{side length}_{1\text{smaller}} &= 7.5 \text{ inches } \\
   \text{side length}_{2\text{larger}} &= \text{side length}_{1\text{smaller}}  \\
   \text{Perimeter of larger equilateral triangle} &= 4 \cdot \text{side length}_{2\text{larger}} \\
   \text{Perimeter of larger equilateral triangle} &= 12 \cdot \text{side length}_{1\text{smaller}} \\
   \text{Perimeter of larger equilateral triangle} &= 90 \text{ inches }
 \end{align*}

\item \begin{equation}
   \begin{aligned}
   &\text{Area of smaller equilateral triangle is: } \\
   & A_1 = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \cdot (\text{side length}_{1\text{smaller}})^2      \\
   & A_1 = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \cdot (7.5 \text{ inches })^2 = 9 \sqrt{3} \text{ square inches }
   \end{aligned}
 \end{equation}

\item \begin{equation}
   \begin{aligned}
   &\text{Area of larger equilateral triangle is: } \\
   &A_2 = \text{Perimeter of larger equilateral triangle}^2 \div (4 \cdot \sqrt{3}) \text{ (using Heron's formula)} \\
   &A_2 = \frac{90^2}{4 \cdot \sqrt{3}} \\
   &A_2 = 144 \sqrt{3} \text{ square inches }
   \end{aligned}
 \end{equation}

\item  \boxed{ 144 \sqrt{3} \text{ square inches } }
\end{enumerate}
\end{document}
```

