The sum of the perimeters of two equilateral triangles is 45 inches. If the side length of the smaller triangle is \(a\), the side length of the larger one is \(16a\). The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is given by \(3 \times \text{side length}\), so we have:
\begin{align}
2a + 16a &= 45 \\
18a &= 45 \\
a &= \frac{45}{18} = \frac{15}{6} = \frac{5}{2} \text{ inches}
\end{align}

Now we can find the areas:
