s &= \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{5}{2} \times \sqrt{\frac{25}{4}} = \frac{25\sqrt{2}}{16} \text{ square inches} \\
l &= \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times 16\frac{5}{2} \times \sqrt{\frac{225}{4}} = 30\sqrt{30} \text{ square inches}
\end{align}

However, it is asked to express the answer in the simplest radical form. \(30\sqrt{30}\) is already in simplest radical form, since \((30\sqrt{30})^2 = 900\times 30 = 27000\), which is a perfect square (since \(27000 = 1728^2\)). The square root of \(1728\) is \(\sqrt{1728} = 42\sqrt{2}\), and so we have:

\begin{equation}
\boxed{30\sqrt{30} = 42\sqrt{2}\sqrt{30}}
\end{equation}

Since \(42\sqrt{2}\) and \(\sqrt{30}\) cannot be simplified further, this represents the simplest radical form for the area of the larger triangle.

