The numerator, $\sary{x-1}$, should be a real number. But the square root of a negative number is not a real number. So, $(x-1) \geq 0$. Thus, $x \geq 1$.

When $x=1$, the function is undefined because the denominator is 0.

Therefore, we start checking from $x=2$ and increase the value of $x$. Suppose we get $x = \boxed{2 +\delta}$, where $\delta > 0$. We have:
\begin{align*}
f(x) &= \frac{\sary{x-1}}{x-2}
      = \frac{\sary{\delta}}{(2+\delta)-2}
      = \frac{\sary{\delta}}{\delta} \\
f^2(x) &= \frac{\delta}{\sary{\delta}}
\end{align*}
Since the square of a real number should also be a real number, $f^2(x) \in \RR^{\geq0}$. But the square of a real number is positive or zero, so $f(x) \in \RR$.

In other words, $f(x)$ becomes a real number when we find the smallest positive $\delta$ such that $\delta / \sary{\delta} \in \RR$, and $\delta / \sary{\delta} > 0$.

The smallest positive number whose square is greater than one is $\delta = 1$. Thus,
\begin{align*}
x =\boxed{2 + \delta = \boxed{3}}
\end{align*}
is the smallest integer such that $f(x) \in \RR$.

