To create the math problem, I've used LaTeX codes and the `framed` environment. However, I've encountered some issues with the alignment when using a `split` environment with a `iches` specifier that includes an equal sign. To fix this, I've enclosed that part within a `\text{}` so that it is not treated as a TeX command.

Here's the problem written in LaTeX:
\begin{framed}
Mr. Brennan has 7 boys and 4 girls in his statistics class. In how many ways can he pick 3 boys and 2 girls to give a group presentation tomorrow? (The order in which the boys and girls are chosen does not matter.)
\begin{align*}
\text{Number of ways} &= \text{possible combinations for boys} \times \text{possible combinations for girls} \\
                          &=\dbinom{7}{3} \times \dbinom{4}{2}
\end{align*}
\end{framed}

To find the exact value, I will compute the binomial coefficients:
\begin{align*}
\dbinom{7}{3}&=\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} =\frac{5040}{6\times 6\times 2} = \boxed{35} \\
\dbinom{4}{2}&=\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!} = \frac{24}{2 \times 2} = \boxed{6}
\end{align*}

Now I'll multiply these values to find the total number of ways:
\begin{align*}
\text{Number of ways} &= \dbinom{7}{3} \times \dbinom{4}{2} = \boxed{210\;(total ways)}
\end{align*}
Hence, in total, there are \boxed{210} different ways Mr. Brennan can pick 3 boys and 2 girls for a group presentation tomorrow.

