This problem is asking for the volume ($V$) of a cylinder given its lateral surface area ($A$). We can use the formula for the surface area of a cylinder ($2\pi r(h + r)$) to solve for the radius ($r$)(thanks to the Cylinder formula help): $r = (A/(2\pi)) - (h/2)$.

Plug this expression for $r$ into the formula for the volume ($V = \pi r^2 h$) and do the substitution:

\boxed{ V = \pi [ (A/(2\pi)) - (h/2) ]^2 h }

Expanding that gives:

\boxed{ V = \pi \left[ \frac{A^2}{4\pi^2} - \frac{Ah}{2\pi} + (h/2)^2 \right]h }

\boxed{ V = \pi \left[ \frac{A^2}{4\pi^2} h^2 - \frac{Ah}{\pi} h + (h^3/4) \right] }

Finally, substitute $h=6\text{ cm}$:

\boxed{ V = \pi \left[ \frac{(48\pi)^2 (6)^2}{4\pi^2} (6) - \frac{(48\pi) (6)}{3\pi} (6) + \frac{(6)^3}{4} \right] }

Doing that gives:

\boxed{ V = 36^3 \pi - 384 \cdot 6\pi + (288) \pi }

Simplifying:

\boxed{ V = (36^3 \pi - 2304\pi + 288\pi)}

\boxed{V = 26176\pi \text{ cubic centimeters} }

