Here we go:

1. Calculate the volume of the original cube: \begin{aligned}
	V &= (base \times height \times depth) && \text{(Formula for the volume of a cube)}\\
	V &= 2\, \text{cm} \times 2\, \text{cm} \times 2\, \text{cm}
\end{aligned}

2. Calculate the new volume of the increased cube:

\begin{aligned}
	V &= \boxed{\color{blue} {2^3}} \times \text{units} \\
	&= 8\, \text{ cubic cm} \times \text{units}
\end{aligned}

The original volume plus the 19 cubic centimeters is the new volume:

\begin{aligned}
	(\boxed{2^3} + 19) \times \text{units} &= 8\, \text{ cubic cm} \times \text{units}\\
	6.01 \times \text{units} &= 8\, \text{ cubic cm} \times \text{units}\\
	\text{units} &= \boxed{\frac{8 \times \text{units}}{6.01}} = \boxed{\frac 8 6\, \text{ units}}
\end{aligned}
After this, substituting the length of one edge in the Volume Formula and defining the new edge length as `e`,

\begin{aligned}
	\boxed{(e)^3} &= (2\, \text{cm})^3 + 19 \times (2\, \text{cm})^3 && \text{(Formula for the volume of a cube)}\\
	8\, \text{ cubic cm} + 19 \times 8 \, \text{ cubic cm} &= (e)^3\\
	\boxed{272 } &= (e)^3 && \text{(Because the total volume increased by } 19 \times \boxed{2^3} \text{ was 272 cubic centimeters)}
\end{aligned}

By taking the cube root of both sides:

\boxed{e} = \boxed{\sqrt[\Large 3]{272}} \text{ cubic cm} = \boxed{8} \text{ centimeters}

As a final note: \boxed{8 \, \text{ cm}}

completions_8:
First, compute the volume of the original cube.
\begin{align*}
V_\text{original} & = (\text{length})^3 \quad \text{ The volume of a cube is given by the cube of its length} \\
& = (2\, \text{cm})^3 \\
& = 8\,\text{cm}^3
\end{align*}

Next, subtract the volume of the original cube from the total volume to find the volume of the new cube:
\begin{align*}
V_\text{new} - V_\text{original} & = 19\, \text{cu cm}
\end{align*}

Now, compute the volume of the new cube:
\begin{align*}
V_\text{new} & = V_\text{original} + (V_\text{new} - V_\text{original}) \\
& = 8\, \text{cu cm} + 19\, \text{cu cm} \\
& = 27\, \text{cu cm}
\end{align*}

To determine the length of one edge of the new cube, take the cube root of the volume:
\begin{align*}
\text{Length of new cube} & = \sqrt[3]{V_\text{new}} \quad \text{ The length of a cube is given by the cube root of its volume} \\
& = \sqrt[3]{27\,\text{cu cm}}
\end{align*}

The result is a cube root (not a rational number), so simplify it:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt[3]{27\,\text{cu cm}} & = 3\,\sqrt[3]{3}\,\text{cu cm} \quad\text{(The cubic root of 27 is } 3^3 = 27\text{ and the cube root of 3 is }\sqrt[3]{3} \text{.)}
\end{align*}

Now, express the length in centimeters:
\begin{align*}
\boxed{\text{Length of new cube}} & = 3\,\sqrt[3]{3}\,\text{cu cm } \text{ units }
\end{align*}

So, the length of one edge of the new cube is \boxed{ 3\,\sqrt[3]{3}\, \text{ cm }}.

